@page "/"

<PageTitle>Home – LLCP Blazor Server Sample</PageTitle>

<h1>List &amp; Label Cross Platform – Blazor Server Sample</h1>

<p>
    This sample demonstrates how to use
    <strong>List &amp; Label Cross Platform (LLCP)</strong> in a
    <strong>Blazor Web App with interactive server rendering</strong>.
</p>

<h2>Architecture</h2>
<ul>
    <li>
        <strong>Server-side rendering</strong> — LLCP and SkiaSharp run on the
        ASP.NET Core server.
    </li>
    <li>
        The report layout is built <strong>programmatically via the DOM API</strong>
        (no <code>.lst</code> or <code>.json</code> design file needed).
        See <code>Services/ReportService.cs</code> for details.
    </li>
    <li>
        Customer data lives in an <strong>in-memory list</strong> provided by
        <code>Services/CustomerDataService.cs</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
        The generated PDF is streamed from the server via a dedicated
        <strong>minimal API endpoint</strong> (<code>GET /api/report/customer.pdf</code>)
        and downloaded by the browser.
    </li>
    <li>
        Blazor components that need interactivity (button clicks, state updates)
        carry the <code>@@rendermode InteractiveServer</code> directive.  These
        components are rendered on the server and kept in sync with the browser
        via a <strong>SignalR</strong> connection.
    </li>
</ul>

<h2>How the PDF Download Works</h2>
<ol>
    <li>The user clicks <strong>Download Report</strong> on the Report page.</li>
    <li>
        The Blazor component (running server-side) calls
        <code>ReportService.GenerateCustomerReport()</code>, which:
        <ul>
            <li>Builds the project layout in a <code>MemoryStream</code> using the LLCP DOM API.</li>
            <li>Exports the report to PDF using a second <code>MemoryStream</code>.</li>
        </ul>
    </li>
    <li>
        The PDF bytes are returned as a <code>byte[]</code>.
        The minimal API endpoint at <code>/api/report/customer.pdf</code>
        streams them to the browser with a <code>Content-Disposition: attachment</code>
        header, triggering a file download.
    </li>
</ol>

<p>
    Head to the <a href="report">Report</a> page to generate a sample PDF.
</p>
